@page "/avatar"

<CatHead Title="Avatars">
    Who did something, or what did. An avatar carries an image, initials or an icon — and never
    the name on its own.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Avatar.Avatars" Title="Sizes and fallbacks" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Three sizes &mdash; <code>.avatar-sm</code>, the default and <code>.avatar-lg</code> &mdash;
        holding an <code>&lt;img&gt;</code>, initials or a Remix Icon, in that order of preference;
        <code>.avatar--muted</code> is for an actor with no person behind it, and
        <code>.avatar--viz-1</code> to <code>.avatar--viz-6</code> give each person in a group
        their own hue from the series tokens &mdash; set the colour with the class, not a
        <code>style</code>, or the initials keep the brand ink and vanish on the fill.
        <strong>An avatar is never the only carrier of a name:</strong> put the name in text beside
        it, in an <code>alt</code>, or in a <code>.visually-hidden</code> span.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Avatar.AvatarGroup" Title="Group" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Overlapping, for &ldquo;these several people&rdquo;. The counter at the end is
        <code>.avatar-more</code> and not an <code>.avatar</code> &mdash; it is a number, not one
        more person.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Avatar.AvatarPair" Title="Pair" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.avatar-pair</code> stacks two people corner to corner inside one avatar's square, so
        in a list a pair's name starts where a single person's does and the column of names stays
        straight &mdash; an <code>.avatar-group</code> there would be as wide as its count.
        <a href="/chat">Chat</a>'s conversation list uses it for a group. Put the size class on the
        pair, not on the avatars inside it; at <code>.avatar-sm</code> one initial fits. Name the
        pair once, on the wrapper, and hide the avatars inside from a screen reader.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Avatar.Presence" Title="Presence" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.avatar-presence</code> around an avatar puts the state on its corner:
        <code>--online</code>, <code>--away</code>, <code>--busy</code>,
        <code>--do-not-disturb</code>, <code>--meeting</code> (in a meeting or a call),
        <code>--presenting</code>, <code>--out-of-office</code>, <code>--offline</code> or
        <code>--unknown</code>. <strong>Every state is a different shape</strong>, not only a
        different colour, so it survives the colour-blind palette and forced colours &mdash; do not
        restyle one into a plain dot. The mark grows with <code>.avatar-lg</code>. It is the
        state's only carrier for the eye and none for a screen reader: name it, on the wrapper's
        <code>aria-label</code> or in the text beside it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Avatar.PresenceLabel" Title="Presence as text, and last seen" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.avatar-presence--label</code> puts the same mark in front of text: in a list row, a
        people picker or a profile card, where the state is written out and can carry a time
        &mdash; &ldquo;in a meeting until 11:30&rdquo;. Offline has nothing more to say than when
        the person was last there, so say that instead, in a <code>&lt;time&gt;</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Avatar.Rings" Title="Rings" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.avatar--ring-warn</code> says an administrator is acting as this person, and
        <code>.avatar--ring</code>, in the brand colour, marks the selected person or the one
        speaking. The ring stands a gap off the avatar and the gap is transparent, so it reads on a
        card, on the page and over a selected row; it works at every size, on an
        <code>.avatar-pair</code>, and under a presence mark. A ring is never the only carrier:
        write &ldquo;Acting as&rdquo; beside it. Do not ring an avatar inside an
        <code>.avatar-group</code>, whose cut-out clips it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Avatar.ActingAsRow" Title="Acting as, in a list" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The person being acted as, in an administrator's user list: the ring on the avatar and a
        <a href="/badge"><code>.badge-warn</code></a> that says so in words.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Avatar.ActingAsTopbar" Title="Acting as, in the topbar" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
    <p>
        While an administrator acts as somebody, the <a href="/topbar">user widget</a> shows that
        person, with <code>.avatar--ring-warn</code> on <code>.user-avatar</code> and the badge in
        place of the e-mail line &mdash; frame classes as they are, nothing added to the frame.
        The menu says who is really signed in and offers the way back. On a phone the topbar
        drops the name and the badge, so name the state on the trigger's
        <code>aria-label</code> as well.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Avatar.Lockup" Title="Name with a subtext" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        An avatar, a name and a second line, with something trailing. There is no
        <code>.person</code> class for this: it is <a href="/list"><code>.list-row</code></a>
        with <code>.list-main</code>, <code>.list-title</code>, <code>.list-sub</code> and
        <code>.list-meta</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Avatar.LockupInATable" Title="In a table cell" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.list-row--flush</code> drops the row's own padding for the places that already
        provide it &mdash; a table cell, a card header, a form row. The access column is a
        <a href="/menu"><code>.value-trigger</code></a>: use it where a
        <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> would be wrong because the options need icons or a description.
    </p>
</CatExample>
